07421875, 0.0254058837890625, -0.01422882080078125, 0.007232666015625, 0.0053253173828125, 0.0162811279296875, 0.027435302734375, 0.054718017578125, -0.0003476142883300781, 0.00911712646484375, -0.02545166015625, -0.0287017822265625, 0.037506103515625, -0.019134521484375, 0.0310211181640625, -0.00027060508728027344, -0.042327880859375, 0.001087188720703125, -6.35385513305664e-05, -0.0019168853759765625, -0.0036830902099609375, 0.033935546875, -0.01168060302734375, 0.00463104248046875, -0.008636474609375, 0.0008988380432128906, -0.007720947265625, 0.00250244140625, 0.01506805419921875, -0.01084136962890625, -0.005153656005859375, 0.003787994384765625, -0.01641845703125, 0.0125579833984375, -0.00226593017578125, -0.0110931396484375, 0.0210113525390625, -0.02032470703125, 0.0019350051879882812, 0.007732391357421875, 0.0160369873046875, 0.017120361328125, -0.042724609375, -0.01297760009765625, -0.0040130615234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and how does human direction of the task remain visible and accountable? This issue is practically significant for organizations and policymakers, as the ability to trace and audit human input is essential for ensuring responsibility, compliance, and trust in AI-mediated processes.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\n- **Human direction**: Any form of human input that shapes, constrains, or corrects the AI\u2019s actions.\n- **Governance problem**: The challenge of ensuring that human oversight and intent are transparent and traceable in AI-mediated workflows.\n- **Observable traces**: Detectable marks or records of human intervention within the workflow\u2014such as logs of instructions, edits, or corrections\u2014which are crucial for accountability and transparency. For example, a timestamped instruction or a revision log constitutes an observable trace, while the absence of such records can obscure responsibility.\n\n**Operational Categories of Human Direction**\n\nWe distinguish two operational routes for human direction:\n\n1. **Specified Delegation**: Human input provided before AI execution, such as explicit instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task.\n   - *Clear example*: \"Summarize this report in 200 words, focusing on financial outcomes.\"\n   - *Edge case*: Uploading a template with embedded instructions. If the template contains explicit, machine-readable instructions (e.g., fields labeled \"exclude regulatory issues\"), this counts as specified delegation. If instructions are implicit or ambiguous (e.g., formatting cues without clear directives), classification depends on whether the AI demonstrably interprets these cues as constraints.\n   - *Decision rule*: An intervention is classified as specified delegation if it occurs prior to AI execution and contains explicit, actionable guidance that the AI can process.\n   - *Additional example*: Selecting a pre-set workflow in an API dashboard that automatically applies certain filters or constraints before task execution.\n   - *Decision aid*: If the human input is present before the AI acts and could be independently audited as a directive, it is specified delegation.\n\n2. **Iterative Coproduction**: Human interventions during or after the AI\u2019s production process, involving review, correction, or redirection of provisional outputs.\n   - *Clear example*: After receiving a draft, a user instructs, \"Revise this section for clarity.\"\n   - *Edge case*: A user makes minor edits (e.g., correcting typos) without explicit feedback. If the intervention alters substantive content or prompts the AI to generate a new output, it is classified as iterative coproduction; otherwise, it may be excluded.\n   - *Decision rule*: An intervention is classified as iterative coproduction if it modifies or redirects the AI\u2019s output after initial execution, regardless of whether feedback is explicit, provided the change is substantive.\n   - *Additional example*: Highlighting a paragraph and clicking \"regenerate\" in a chat interface after reviewing the initial output.\n   - *Decision aid*: If the human input occurs after the AI\u2019s initial output and results in a meaningful change or new output, it is iterative coproduction.\n\n*For ambiguous cases, a decision tree is used: (1) Did the human input occur before or after AI execution? (2) Was the input explicit and actionable? (3) Did the input result in a substantive change or new output? Classification follows the above rules based on these answers.*\n\n**Research Question**\n\nThis article addresses: **How does the orientation of human-AI interaction\u2014specifically, work-related versus personal use\u2014affect the timing and form of human direction in AI-mediated tasks?** This distinction matters because organizations may require robust audit trails for compliance, while personal use may prioritize flexibility or privacy.\n\n---\n\n**Data and Methodology**\n\n*Data Source and Construction*\n\n- **Data Source**: Aggregate monthly data from the **Anthropic Economic Index (AEI)** for April and May 2026. The AEI is a composite measure constructed from anonymized logs of human-AI interactions across Anthropic\u2019s platforms.\n- **Variables included**: For each interaction, the AEI records user ID (anonymized), timestamp, platform/mode (1P API or Claude.ai), context tag (work or personal), task type (mapped to O*NET categories), instruction content, intervention logs (e.g., edits, feedback), and metadata on task completion.\n- **Nature of AI-mediated tasks**: Measured by mapping user-supplied task descriptions and interaction logs to standardized Level-0 O*NET occupational activities.\n- **Context tag validation**: Tags are user-supplied at the start of each session or inferred from metadata (e.g., organizational account vs. personal account). For analysis, tags were cleaned by removing records with missing or conflicting tags and cross-checking with account type where possible.\n\n*Sample Selection*\n\n- **Coverage**: Millions of records from users who consented to logging, filtered for completeness and metadata quality. The dataset spans a diverse user base but is limited to Anthropic\u2019s ecosystem.\n- **Classification of Use**: The AEI distinguishes between work-related and personal uses based on user-supplied context tags and interaction metadata. Tags may be inconsistently applied or ambiguous (e.g., a side project tagged as \"work\" or missing tags), introducing potential misclassification.\n- **Modes of Use**:\n  - **1P API**: Direct traffic through Anthropic's API, typically used by organizations in structured workflows.\n  - **Claude.ai**: Aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces, reflecting more interactive and conversational uses.\n\n*Task Categorization and Inclusion Criteria*\n\n- **Level-0 O*NET tasks**: Standardized occupational activities used to categorize the nature of tasks delegated to AI.\n- **Eligible records**: Data entries with sufficient metadata and clear task categorization in both modes.\n- **Node-month pairs**: Each pair represents a unique combination of a user node (individual or organizational account) and a calendar month. Only node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes are included (n = 1,411), ensuring comparability but excluding users or periods with single-mode activity.\n\n*Simulation Design*\n\n- **Rationale**: To estimate how the profiles of specified delegation and iterative coproduction change under a controlled shift in use orientation, minimizing confounding from other variables.\n- **Procedure**:\n  - For each eligible node-month pair, identify the proportion of tasks tagged as personal and work-related.\n  - Simulate reallocating 10 percentage points of task volume from personal use to work-related use within each node-month pair. The reallocation is performed proportionally across all personal-use tasks, selected at random within the pair, and reclassified as work-related, holding all other variables constant.\n  - After reallocation, recalculate the rates of specified delegation and iterative coproduction for each mode.\n  - Statistical inference: Compute mean differences and 95% confidence intervals using paired t-tests across node-month pairs.\n- **Assumptions**: The simulation assumes that tasks shifted from personal to work-related use are otherwise comparable in content, complexity, and user characteristics. This may not fully account for unobserved differences (e.g., user expertise, task complexity).\n\n*Handling of Confounders and Robustness*\n\n- **Observed controls**: The analysis is restricted to node-month pairs with activity in both modes, controlling for user and temporal effects.\n- **Sensitivity analysis**: As a robustness check, the simulation was repeated excluding node-month pairs with high variance in task complexity (as proxied by O*NET category diversity), yielding similar results (see Supplementary Table 1).\n- **Limitations**: Unmeasured confounders such as user expertise and true task complexity remain; future work should collect more granular data on these variables.\n\n---\n\n**Results**\n\n*Specified Delegation*\n\n- Increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) when shifting toward work-related use.\n- Relative to baseline rates (mean specified delegation of 18.2 in 1P API and 12.7 in Claude.ai), these represent increases of approximately 15% and 11%, respectively.\n- *Practical significance*: In a typical organizational workflow, a 2.76-point increase could mean that, for every 100 tasks, nearly three more are accompanied by explicit, auditable instructions\u2014potentially improving traceability and compliance documentation.\n\n*Iterative Coproduction*\n\n- Changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]).\n- This suggests iterative, in-process human interventions are more prevalent in conversational Claude.ai mode than in the structured 1P API mode.\n\n*Potential Confounders*\n\n- While observable characteristics at the node-month level are controlled, unmeasured factors such as user expertise (e.g., novice vs. expert users) and task complexity (e.g., routine vs. novel tasks) may influence both the mode of interaction and the form of human direction. Sensitivity analyses restricting to more homogeneous task types yielded similar patterns, but more granular data are needed for definitive conclusions.\n\n---\n\n**Implications for Policy and Practice**\n\n- **Structured API deployments**: Organizations should implement audit mechanisms that automatically log and archive upfront specifications (e.g., instruction templates, parameter files). Audit tools should include features such as version control, timestamped change logs, and automated alerts for missing or ambiguous instructions.\n- **Conversational interfaces**: Develop tools that track and timestamp in-process interventions (e.g., edit histories, feedback logs). Features should include searchable intervention logs, user attribution, and the ability to reconstruct the sequence of human-AI exchanges for post-hoc auditing.\n- **Training interventions**: Provide user training tailored to each mode\u2014emphasizing the importance of explicit instructions in API"}}